\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The maximal prolongation has square zero}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring, let #{X} be a finite set, and
let #{\Omega^1} be a free calculus in the sense of \ref{fdip-00NU},
with derivation #{d:A\to\Omega^1}. Form the tensor algebra of the
bimodule #{\Omega^1} over #{A}. The \newvocab{maximal prolongation}
is the largest graded algebra
##{A\oplus\Omega^1\oplus\Omega^2\oplus\cdots}
generated by #{A} and #{\Omega^1}, with the given actions of #{A},
which carries an extension of #{d} to a graded derivation of degree
#{1}. In that extension
##{d(f\,dg)=df\,dg}
for all #{f,g\in A}. Products of #{1}-forms obey only the relations
forced by the vanishing already present in #{\Omega^1} and by the
graded Leibniz rule. The identity #{d^2=0} follows from that rule
on every degree, and it is not imposed separately.}

\proof{
\p{The displayed formula gives
#{d(df)=d(1\cdot df)=d(1)\,df=0}, since #{d(1)=0}. Thus #{d^2}
vanishes on functions. Suppose #{d^2} vanishes on two forms
#{\alpha} and #{\beta}, of degrees #{r} and #{s}. Graded Leibniz
gives
##{\begin{aligned}
d^2(\alpha\beta)
&=d\bigl(d\alpha\cdot\beta+(-1)^r\alpha\cdot d\beta\bigr)\\
&=d^2\alpha\cdot\beta+(-1)^{r+1}d\alpha\cdot d\beta\\
&\quad+(-1)^r d\alpha\cdot d\beta+(-1)^{r+s}\alpha\cdot d^2\beta.
\end{aligned}}
The two middle signs are opposite, and the outer terms vanish by
the inductive hypothesis. Every form in the prolongation is a sum of
products of functions and elements #{df}, so #{d^2=0} on the whole
algebra.}
}

\p{The same square-zero extension is the reduction of the universal
envelope by a digraph in \citef{dimakis1994graphs}.
The two calculations that follow specialise it.}
